## Part 4: Logic Error
```java
public class TemperaturePractice {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double celsius = 35;
        double fahrenheit = (9 / 5) * celsius + 32;

        System.out.println("Celsius: " + celsius);
        System.out.println("Fahrenheit: " + fahrenheit);
    }
}


This is incorrect. 35°C should convert to 95°F, not 67°F, because 9 / 5 is integer division in Java and truncates to 1, instead of 1.8.

```java
public class TemperaturePractice {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double celsius = 35;
        double fahrenheit = (9.0 / 5.0) * celsius + 32;

        System.out.println("Celsius: " + celsius);
        System.out.println("Fahrenheit: " + fahrenheit);
    }
}

## Part 5: Classify the Errors

Scenario A — Syntax error (missing semicolon)
Scenario B — Logic error (wrong formula, but still runs and shows a result)
Scenario C — Runtime error (division by zero during execution)
Scenario D — Syntax error (unclosed string literal)
Scenario E — Logic error (wrong calculation, program still runs)

